  "query_preview": "(This evening through Thursday)...\nIssued at 248 PM EDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nTonight through Sunday...\n\nA broad area of surface high pressure remains centered over Ontario \nCanada. A surface ridge axis extends southward into the Gulf \nshunting deep moisture well south of central Indiana. Meanwhile, \nwidespread sinking motion on the eastern edge of upper ridging over \nthe plains will continue to support mostly clear skies. \nReinforcements to the ridge of surface high pressure will occur as \nadditional shortwave energy dives southward into the eastern \nseaboard through Sunday. As a result several more days of very \nquiescent weather is expected for central Indiana, with highs in the \nlow to mid 70s and light winds. Clear skies, a dry airmass and \nfairly long nights will continue the stretch of large diurnal temp \nswings with lows in the low-mid 40s. More late night/early morning \npatchy fog is also expected in low-lying and traditionally colder \nspots thanks to ideal radiational cooling conditions. \n\nMonday through Wednesday...\n\nUpper ridging will breakdown over the MS valley as shortwave energy \nfrom the western CONUS trough moves eastward into the plains on \nMonday. Despite increasing high cloud cover, guidance may be \nunderdoing high temps on Monday, given max thickness values in the \nmid 560s. Although some increasing ascent will occur as the \ndeamplifying shortwave trough approaches the MS valley and central \nIndiana on Tue/Wed, only an increase in mid level clouds is \nexpected. The surface ridge axis will remain well entrenched over \nthe Gulf, effectively cutting off any moisture return despite \nsoutherly BL flow developing. The biggest change to the weather \nbesides the increase in cloud cover will be increase in overnight \nlows to upper 40s/low 50s Tuesday and Wednesday mornings. \n\nThursday and Beyond...\n\nMedium range models are in less agreement with the extended period \nwith main differences centered on phasing between shortwave \nenergy moving southeastward through Canada and an upper low/trough\nover the PAC NW moving eastward towards the northern plains. As a\nresult, the timing of precip is in question. In line with the \nGFS/GEFS members, the NBM has negligible PoPs through Friday but \nthen opens up the gates for the weekend. While the Op Euro/Euro- \nAIFS and the GFS-AI have a frontal boundary generating precip \nacross the area on Thursday. Beyond the forecast time period \nuncertainty grows but there is an increasing composite signal for\nwetter than normal weather beginning around Halloween into early \nNovember.",
